=== Muscle Cramps and Contractures
<subsec:cramps>

*Clinical Presentation.*

    - Spontaneous muscle cramps without preceding exertion
    - Nocturnal leg cramps
    - Cramps in unexpected muscle groups (hands, feet, neck, throat, jaw)
    - Prolonged muscle contractures
    - Reverse finger contractures (fingers held extended rather than curled)
    - Difficulty releasing grip or relaxing contracted muscles
    - Constant sensation of being "ready to cramp"

*Mechanism.*
Muscle relaxation requires ATP to pump calcium ions back into storage. When ATP is in\-sufficient, muscles cannot fully relax, leading to spontaneous cramping. This reflects the same energy deficit causing fatigue, but mani\-fested as impaired muscle relaxation.

#open-question(title: [Neurogenic vs Muscular Origin of Spontaneous Cramps])[
Two fundamentally different mechanisms could explain spontaneous, exertion-independent cramps in ME/CFS, and the available evidence does not yet distinguish them.

*(Certainty: n/a — competing hypotheses, unresolved.)*

*Neurogenic (motor-nerve-terminal hyperexcitability).* In the general population, spontaneous cramps are most often traced to hyperexcitability of the motor nerve terminal driven by ion channels — increased HCN-channel inward rectification in cramp-fasciculation syndrome @Czesnik2015HCN, ectopic motor-axon discharges in peripheral-nerve hyperexcitability syndromes, and sustained abnormal spinal reflex activity @Maughan2019MuscleCramping. Sensory-nerve TRP channels can gate this motor-neuron cramp threshold @Craighead2017TRPcramp. If ME/CFS cramps share this origin, treatment would follow the neurogenic route (e.g., carbamazepine or gabapentin), as in the general literature.

*Muscular (sarcolemmal depolarization).* The only published ME/CFS-specific account of cramps and fasciculations proposes a muscular mechanism: Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase dysfunction → sarcolemmal depolarization → hyperexcitability, and explicitly excludes a neuronal cause @WirthSteinacker2025fasciculations. This is consistent with the muscle sodium overload documented in ME/CFS.

*Discriminating evidence.* Magnesium does not prevent cramps in a Cochrane meta-analysis @Garrison2020MagnesiumCramps — arguing against a simple ion-deficit explanation regardless of which mechanism dominates. Small-fiber neuropathy, present in ME/CFS, is associated with cramps in the general population via intramuscular-nerve excitation @Lopate2013CrampsSFN, and could bridge either model.

*Falsifiable discriminator:* If cramps are neurogenic, nerve-conduction/EMG studies would show ectopic motor-unit discharges or abnormal axonal excitability (e.g., raised HCN-driven inward rectification on threshold tracking); if muscular, they would show normal nerve function but sarcolemmal membrane abnormalities. No ME/CFS-specific study has yet performed this discrimination.

*Severity applicability:* unknown — not stratified in the general-population studies.

*Consequence:* Whether a patient's cramps are best treated as a nerve-excitability problem (anti-seizure medication) or as a muscle-membrane problem changes the treatment choice; this is currently an unanswered, testable question rather than an established finding.
] <oq:neurogenic-vs-muscular-cramp>

#speculation(title: [Motor-Nerve Hyperexcitability as a Contributor to ME/CFS Spontaneous Cramps])[
*(Origin: brainstorm.)* *(Certainty: 0.30 — general-population mechanism, untested in ME/CFS.)* Spontaneous, exertion-independent cramps in ME/CFS might partly reflect motor-nerve-terminal hyperexcitability rather than (or in addition to) the energy-deficit model above. In the general population this is the default mechanism — ion-channel (HCN, slow K⁺) driven @Czesnik2015HCN, sometimes associated with small-fiber neuropathy exciting intramuscular nerves @Lopate2013CrampsSFN. ME/CFS patients show small-fiber neuropathy, so a neurogenic contribution to their cramps is plausible but unproven.

*Falsifiable prediction:* In ME/CFS patients with spontaneous cramps, threshold-tracking axonal excitability studies would show elevated HCN-driven inward rectification (as in cramp-fasciculation syndrome) compared with cramp-free ME/CFS patients. Falsified if ME/CFS cramp patients show normal axonal excitability or a purely sarcolemmal abnormality.

*Limitations:* Zero ME/CFS-specific empirical support for the neurogenic mechanism; the only ME/CFS-specific published account favors a muscular origin @WirthSteinacker2025fasciculations; transcranial magnetic stimulation studies in ME/CFS show reduced, not increased, central motor-cortical excitability @Starr2000TMS — arguing against a *central* hyperexcitability contribution even if a peripheral-nerve component were present.

*Severity applicability:* unknown — general-population mechanism not stratified by ME/CFS severity.

*Consequence:* If replicated, this would reframe ME/CFS cramps as a treatable nerve-excitability problem (carbamazepine/gabapentin) rather than purely an energy-deficit symptom — but the evidence is currently too weak to change practice.
] <spec:motor-nerve-hyperexcitability-cramp>
